i c: language becomes an actuator, not just a representation

The strongest non-mythic reading of i c is that language has two roles:

Language_{descriptive}

and:

Language_{causal}.

A phrase can describe a state, but it can also modify the listener’s state.

So the full interface is:

World
\rightarrow
word
\rightarrow
receiver\ representation
\rightarrow
receiver\ action
\rightarrow
World'.

This fits the earlier Narrative Geometry doctrine that representations are lossy descriptions and possible actuators.

Pantheon should therefore attach a second annotation to language artifacts:

semantic_content
intended_state_effect

For example, the same proposition can be encoded to inform, calm, motivate, inhibit, orient, or trigger recall.

That is operationally useful for Semantic Router and Seals.

Seals get a cleaner mechanism

