\rightarrow
state/policy\ change.

So a Seal is not “magic shorthand.”

It is a high-compression state-transition cue.

That gives a measurable Seal criterion:

SealQuality =
ReconstructionFidelity
\times
BehavioralUtility
\div
TokenCost.

A strong Seal should reliably reactivate the intended operator or state across contexts without carrying unnecessary prose.

This is a useful repo-ready metric.

Reality Stitch: the Genesis commits need strong ontological separation

gametes and serpent sight are legitimate structural readings, but they should remain classified correctly.

The repository itself already contains the necessary caveat: gametes says the interpretation does not establish historical authorial intent. 

Pantheon should distinguish at least:

TextualObservation

StructuralMapping

HistoricalClaim.

The new material supports the second.

It does not establish the third.

So Atlas metadata should record something like:

interpretation_type: structural / mythopoetic
historical_provenance: unestablished
literal_claim: false/unasserted

This is exactly the discipline developed through Structural Etymology and GIPP:

\boxed{
coherent\ mapping
\neq
historical\ provenance.
}

